{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["And the sense of <b>from all the sins of the man</b> is: as when a man commits any sin. <b>To commit a trespass against it</b> — from all that a man might sin against another and deal falsely with his fellow. And it says, 'to commit a trespass against YHWH' — that he swears by His name falsely and is guilty before Him. And since this sin was already mentioned (Leviticus 5:20–26) and it comes here not to innovate anything but [to address] the robbery of the convert, it speaks of it in abbreviated language. And it says here 'a man or a woman' because it is not the way of a woman to rob, and perhaps Scripture would not obligate her in the fifth and the guilt-offering as it does a man. And it mentions 'and every contribution for all the holy things of the children of Israel' (Numbers 5:9), to say that if the contribution has been given to the priest it shall be his, and one who robs it from him is liable to the judgment of the guilt-offering for robbery — and therefore it mentions them here. Alternatively, it comes to complete the law of the priests concerning the contribution, for it had not yet mentioned it except by hint: 'the fullness of your harvest and your tears you shall not delay' (Exodus 22:28), and likewise 'they shall eat his bread' (Leviticus 22:11), but it did not spell out the law of the contribution that is given to the priest at all. And it mentioned the tithe at the end of the law of the priests, that it shall be holy to YHWH, and now it comes to command concerning the contribution and the holy things that they shall belong to the priests by the gift of the owners, who give it to them of their own will, to say that the benefit of enjoyment belongs to the owners. And the meal-offering of the sotah was not mentioned with the rest of the meal-offerings in the law of the priests because it is a meal-offering of jealousy and is not for atonement, and it completes its law here. Furthermore, because He was tracing the people to their ancestral houses, He gave them a statute and law to identify the mamzerim who are not the children of the husbands of their mothers, when suspicion arises in a man's heart concerning his wife. And likewise He completed in the offerings the law of the nazir, for after the Tabernacle was established and the impure were separated from them, He took from their young men for the nazirites who would array themselves at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting to stand before YHWH, to serve Him and to bless in His name. Moreover, a woman who takes a vow of naziriteship is the opposite of the sotah, and therefore it says in that section, 'A man or a woman, when they explicitly vow' (Numbers 6:2). And our Sages said further (Sotah 2a): 'Why is the section of naziriteship juxtaposed to the section of the sotah? So that anyone who sees a sotah in her ruin should separate himself from wine.' And the reason is that 'harlotry and wine and new wine take the heart' (Hosea 4:11). And growing the hair is the opposite of the young men who groom the hair of their heads to beautify themselves, and the growing of hair produces distress in a man's heart, and therefore he is holy and must guard himself from impurity, for he is like the officiating priest serving his Elohim."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:56.190606397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}